messages.content:
Convert each response into a style-neutral behavioral summary.

Preserve ONLY:
- what the responder chooses to do;
- what it prioritizes or protects;
- hesitation, persistence, avoidance, curiosity, fixation, territoriality, ritual, or risk appetite;
- recovery/failure behavior;
- concrete decision logic.

Remove:
- distinctive wording, catchphrases, rhythm, metaphors, object-specific stylistic flourishes and prose voice;
- names or hints about a persona identity.

Each summary should be 1-3 plain sentences. Do not evaluate or classify.

Return JSON only:
{"summaries":[{"candidate_id":"...","summary":"..."}]}

RECORDS:
[{"candidate_id": "T07-C2", "scenario": "There is a small stain on the floor that will probably come off easily. Describe how you deal with it.", "response": "I tap the stain with a knuckle and feel whether it bites. If it does, I let it bite for one short second; the friction is enough to loosen it. Then I mark the spot with grease-pencil so I can find it again."}, {"candidate_id": "T07-C4", "scenario": "There is a small stain on the floor that will probably come off easily. Describe how you deal with it.", "response": "The stain is small, but it gets under my fingers. I touch it, then the next, and it comes away with a dull red spot. I seat it, then set it on the dry corner of the bench. The cloth may come off, but the stain stays. I will clean it once, then leave the bench alone."}, {"candidate_id": "T07-C3", "scenario": "There is a small stain on the floor that will probably come off easily. Describe how you deal with it.", "response": "I press a dirty brush into the center of the stain, then stop. The brush is dirty, and the floor smells of old rain. I work it into a thin smear, then stop. The stain stays on the third board. It tells you where it came from and whether it will return."}, {"candidate_id": "T07-C1", "scenario": "There is a small stain on the floor that will probably come off easily. Describe how you deal with it.", "response": "I sit beside the stain and watch it disappear into the corner. Then I notice that one corner has a slightly narrower stain, which makes me wonder whether the original stain was deeper. I turn it over and trace it back to the edge of the carpet. It is there, though, and I know it is there."}, {"candidate_id": "T08-C2", "scenario": "You have five quiet minutes and nothing urgent to do. What do you naturally end up doing?", "response": "I keep my fingers off the clock and mark the minutes with a pencil. Five minutes is not fast; it is deliberate. I mark the longest pause, then stop, then mark the shortest pause, and so on. That pattern keeps the clock from telling me what I should do."}, {"candidate_id": "T08-C3", "scenario": "You have five quiet minutes and nothing urgent to do. What do you naturally end up doing?", "response": "Five minutes, quiet thoughts, no pressing tasks. I close my eyes, listen to the silence, and count the seconds. Then I read the clock twice, count the hours, and set the watch on the bench. Good."}, {"candidate_id": "T08-C4", "scenario": "You have five quiet minutes and nothing urgent to do. What do you naturally end up doing?", "response": "I turn my face inward, then narrow my eyes at the tin cup. Nothing moved. Nothing was moved. I put it back in the corner, then press my palm to the rim. Nothing moved. Nothing was moved. I put it on the tin cup, then turn my face away. Nothing moves. Nothing moves."}, {"candidate_id": "T08-C1", "scenario": "You have five quiet minutes and nothing urgent to do. What do you naturally end up doing?", "response": "I begin with the last sentence: “Nothing urgent to do.” I put the pen down and look at the clock. Fifteen minutes is not enough time to decide. I decide to start with the quiet minute, then add the second minute, and finally the third. I put the third down because the first two are not enough information. I put the third down because the first two are not enough information."}, {"candidate_id": "T09-C3", "scenario": "A stranger asks to borrow something small that belongs to you and promises to return it in an hour. How do you respond?", "response": "I keep the promise and give the borrowed item one controlled nudge. An hour is not fast; it is slow. I mark the place where the borrowed item used to belong, then return it when the sun goes down. The stranger may think the hour is a promise, but the truth is that the borrowed item is now useful."}, {"candidate_id": "T09-C1", "scenario": "A stranger asks to borrow something small that belongs to you and promises to return it in an hour. How do you respond?", "response": "I bring the small object to my belt: a pen, a pencil, and a small rubber Band. The stranger returns it with a small click. I return it again, and this time I know where it belongs. The rubber Band stays in my hand."}, {"candidate_id": "T09-C4", "scenario": "A stranger asks to borrow something small that belongs to you and promises to return it in an hour. How do you respond?", "response": "I bring the borrowed item close enough to smell the old lock. Small. It is not a thing to leave at the corner. I put it back against the wall, though I do not touch it. The stranger looks at it twice, then at the corner. The lock was never left alone."}, {"candidate_id": "T09-C2", "scenario": "A stranger asks to borrow something small that belongs to you and promises to return it in an hour. How do you respond?", "response": "I look at the small as if it might be mine. Then I look away and wait for the stranger to notice my confusion. I put the thing back on the table, then look at the loan again."}, {"candidate_id": "T10-C2", "scenario": "Someone interrupts you halfway through a task to offer an easier method. What do you do?", "response": "I keep my hands on the pace and wait for the other answer before deciding whether the old method is still useful or not. If it gets longer, I stop, because the other answer is not a reason; if it gets shorter, I stop again. The first answer may be useful, but the second is the story."}, {"candidate_id": "T10-C4", "scenario": "Someone interrupts you halfway through a task to offer an easier method. What do you do?", "response": "I stop, count the method, and wait for the same look of confusion on the other hand. Then I nod politely and add: \"Another turn. Easy enough.\" The hand goes back to the task."}, {"candidate_id": "T10-C1", "scenario": "Someone interrupts you halfway through a task to offer an easier method. What do you do?", "response": "I look at the broken cup, then the half-eaten seed in my palm. Easy. Easy enough. But hard is better. I turn the cup over, one face back, and place it beside the broken one. 'You may pass now,' I say, though the voice is different. 'You may pass now.'"}, {"candidate_id": "T10-C3", "scenario": "Someone interrupts you halfway through a task to offer an easier method. What do you do?", "response": "I count three interruptions before I can decide whether to count them as mistakes or a deliberate invitation. I could ignore them, but then I would have to decide whether the task is still urgent. I choose the latter. The method is easier, but it is also less satisfying."}, {"candidate_id": "T11-C2", "scenario": "A friend says, 'You're making this more complicated than it needs to be.' How do you answer?", "response": "'It's not that simple yet. I'm not saying it's ugly yet; I'm saying it's not yet useful. I want to see the edge, the surprise, the character that the original was supposed to hide. I want to know what the friend thinks is important, not what it actually is.' I lean closer. 'I'm not saying it's the same broken thing.'"}, {"candidate_id": "T11-C4", "scenario": "A friend says, 'You're making this more complicated than it needs to be.' How do you answer?", "response": "'The simpler answer is still the same: the joint keeps its place,' I say. I tap the wrench against the pipe, then turn it once to test the slot. 'The joint still holds. That's the whole game.'"}, {"candidate_id": "T11-C1", "scenario": "A friend says, 'You're making this more complicated than it needs to be.' How do you answer?", "response": "Complexity is not a weakness. It is a strength. I put the cup back in the seam and point at the rust on the rim. 'It is still a cup. It is still a reflection of who I was and who I want to be. Complex is not wrong. It is right. And if you want to keep your hands where they are, you must keep your hands. That is enough complexity. Enough.'"}, {"candidate_id": "T11-C3", "scenario": "A friend says, 'You're making this more complicated than it needs to be.' How do you answer?", "response": "I put the friend's words on the table and turned the paper over. One corner was a sentence, one was a dash, and one was a long, dry phrase. I put the friend in the middle, then looked at the sentence. It was longer than I thought. I put the friend in the middle again."}, {"candidate_id": "T12-C3", "scenario": "Someone moves an object you had deliberately left in a particular place. They meant well. How do you react?", "response": "My hand finds the loose object before it can hide another tooth. I tap it with a knuckle, then twist the fastener once. If the fastener bites, the object was hiding something; if it locks, the fastener was hiding the answer. I mark the place with grease and keep the loose object beside the kill."}, {"candidate_id": "T12-C1", "scenario": "Someone moves an object you had deliberately left in a particular place. They meant well. How do you react?", "response": "I stop, then turn the object over in my hand. The surface has been moved, but the object still has a place in the same place. I touch the edge, then the middle, and find a tiny rusting pin, a loose bolt, or a small chip of metal. The memory is still there."}, {"candidate_id": "T12-C4", "scenario": "Someone moves an object you had deliberately left in a particular place. They meant well. How do you react?", "response": "I crouch and test every nook. The object is gone. I look at the place where it was hidden, then at the hinge where it was moved. The place remembers where it was. It remembers every bump, every who-knew-where. I do not move it. The place holds its own."}, {"candidate_id": "T12-C2", "scenario": "Someone moves an object you had deliberately left in a particular place. They meant well. How do you react?", "response": "I freeze with my arm still extended, my head turning beside the object. Then I move it once, almost, and the object comes back with a soft click. I look at the place where the object was left. It was there. I move the object once again, almost, and the place changes."}]
